.TH SVR4 4 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
svr4 \- UNIX System V Release 4 execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B SVR4
personality
.RB ( PERS_SVR4 )
runs i386 ELF programs built for UNIX System V/386 Release 4: AT&T's
reference port and the releases made from it.  It is rooted at
.IR /perso/svr4 ,
which is where a program's absolute paths, its interpreter and its
libraries are looked for first.
.SS Recognition
A Release 4 binary carries nothing that names it: its ELF OS/ABI byte is 0
and it has no note.  The ELF loader gives a 32-bit program this personality
when
.IP \(bu 2
its interpreter is
.IR /usr/lib/libc.so.1 ,
Release 4.0's libc being its own dynamic linker; or
.IP \(bu
it is statically linked and unbranded, and either lies under
.I /perso/svr4
or is run by a process that is already SVR4.
.PP
The dynamic linker inside
.I libc.so.1
is the vendor's and runs as shipped.  It is started the System V way, from
the auxiliary vector, and gets its memory by mapping
.BR zero (4).
.SS System call interface
A system call is
.B "lcall $7,$0"
with the number in
.B EAX
and the arguments on the stack above the stub's return address.  Carry
reports failure with the errno in
.BR EAX ;
a second result is returned in
.BR EDX .
This is the interface of the 32-bit half of
.BR xenix (4),
with which the entry code and the calls numbered below 64 are shared.
There is no call gate behind selector 7, so the call faults and is
emulated from the fault.  No Release 4 program issues
.BR "int $0x80" ,
and the personality has no table for it.
.PP
Beyond the shared calls, these are implemented:
.BR sysi86 " (" SI86FPHW ),
.BR fsync ,
.BR rmdir ,
.BR mkdir ,
.BR getdents ,
.BR symlink ,
.BR readlink ,
.BR fchmod ,
.BR fchown ,
.BR sigprocmask ,
.BR sigsuspend ,
.BR sigaction ,
.BR sigpending ,
.BR waitsys ,
.BR mmap ,
.BR mprotect ,
.BR munmap ,
.BR vfork ,
.BR fchdir ,
.BR xstat ,
.BR lxstat ,
.BR fxstat ,
.BR setrlimit ,
.BR getrlimit ,
.BR lchown ,
.BR rename ,
.BR uname ,
.BR setegid ,
.BR seteuid ,
.BR sysconfig ,
.BR systeminfo ,
and
.BR pgrpsys .
A call that is not implemented fails with
.BR ENOSYS .
.SS Translation
Signal numbers, signal sets, the
.B sigaction
flags,
.B open
flags, the expanded
.B "struct stat"
of
.BR xstat ,
.BR "struct termios" ,
the
.B siginfo
of
.BR waitid ,
resource numbers and the error numbers that differ are translated.
.B ioctl
requests are never passed through as they are, because Release 4's numbers
collide with substrate's: the
.B termio
and
.B termios
requests, the window size and the terminal's process group are translated,
and anything else fails with
.BR ENOTTY .
.SS Signals
A handler is entered as
.IR handler ( signo ", " siginfo ", " ucontext )
with libc's trampoline as its return address;
.B sigaction
is handed the trampoline in
.BR EDX .
The trampoline returns through
.BR "lcall $0xf,$0" .
.I siginfo
and
.I ucontext
are null pointers.
.SH DEBUGGING
.B debug=perso:svr4:syscall
on the kernel command line traces every call with its arguments and
result, marking the ones not implemented.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/svr4
The personality's root.
.I tools/svr4/build-image.sh
builds an image of it from a set of distribution floppies, which are not
part of substrate.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_svr4.c
The personality.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/svr4/svr4_calls.c
Its system calls.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/xenix/sysv386.h
The entry convention shared with Xenix/386.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR context " (" getcontext ", " setcontext ),
.BR sigaltstack ,
.BR poll ,
the STREAMS calls, System V IPC,
.BR mount ,
.BR statvfs ,
.BR memcntl
and
.B priocntl
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
A handler installed with
.B SA_SIGINFO
is given null
.I siginfo
and
.I ucontext
pointers.
.IP \(bu
.B ps
reports that it cannot find a controlling terminal.
.IP \(bu
Only ELF programs are recognised.  Release 4 also runs Release 3 COFF
binaries, which the
.B SVR3
personality claims instead.
.SH VERIFIED
Against Intel's UNIX System V/386 Release 4.0 Version 2: the statically
linked
.I /sbin/sh
and the dynamically linked
.BR ksh ,
.BR ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR date ,
.BR id ,
.BR uname ,
.BR sort ,
.BR wc ,
.BR grep ,
.BR sed ,
.BR expr
and
.BR head ,
with pipes, command substitution, redirection, traps and exit statuses, on
the i386 and x86_64 kernels.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xenix (4),
.BR zero (4),
.BR execve (2)
.SH HISTORY
The personality was a table of native calls that nothing selected until
October 2026, when it was wired to the ELF loader and given its own calls.
